<h2>Numerical Analysis — Approximating the Unsolvable</h2>
<p style="font-size:.95em;color:var(--text-sec);margin-bottom:16px">Numerical analysis designs, analyzes, and implements algorithms for continuous mathematics. Most real-world problems have no closed-form solution — numerical methods give approximate answers with controlled error. The field spans root-finding, interpolation, numerical integration, ODE solvers, linear algebra, optimization, and spectral methods. The key trade-off is accuracy vs cost.</p>
<div class="quote-box"><div class="quote">"Numerical analysis is the science of computing — turning mathematical theory into practical algorithms."</div><div class="attribution">— Lloyd N. Trefethen</div></div>

<div class="section-label part1">Part 1</div>
<h2>🧮 Root-Finding, Interpolation, and Approximation</h2>
<div class="story-card">
<div class="story-label">Finding Zeros and Fitting Curves</div>
<div class="story-title">From Newton's Method to Chebyshev Polynomials</div>
<div class="story-text"><strong>Newton's method</strong> x_{n+1} = x_n − f(x_n)/f'(x_n) converges quadratically near a simple root. The <strong>secant method</strong> approximates the derivative with finite differences. <strong>Lagrange interpolation</strong> fits a degree-n polynomial through n+1 points — but high-degree interpolation can oscillate wildly (Runge's phenomenon). <strong>Chebyshev nodes</strong> minimize the maximum interpolation error. <strong>Cubic splines</strong> are piecewise cubic polynomials with C² continuity — they avoid oscillation and are the gold standard for smooth interpolation. The <strong>Weierstrass approximation theorem</strong> says any continuous function on [a,b] can be uniformly approximated by polynomials — but in practice, piecewise methods are often better.</div>
<div class="story-moral">Interpolation is the art of guessing what lies between — splines beat polynomials for large datasets.</div>
</div>

<div class="section-label part2">Part 2</div>
<h2>🎯 Numerical Integration and Differentiation</h2>
<div class="story-card">
<div class="story-label">Quadrature and Finite Differences</div>
<div class="story-title">Approximating Integrals — From Newton-Cotes to Gauss Quadrature</div>
<div class="story-text"><strong>Newton-Cotes formulas</strong> (rectangle rule, trapezoidal rule, Simpson's rule) approximate ∫f(x)dx by interpolating f at equally spaced points. Simpson's rule has O(h⁴) accuracy for smooth functions. <strong>Romberg integration</strong> uses Richardson extrapolation to accelerate trapezoidal convergence. <strong>Gaussian quadrature</strong> chooses both nodes and weights to exactly integrate polynomials up to degree 2n−1 with just n points — the nodes are roots of Legendre polynomials. For periodic functions, the <strong>trapezoidal rule</strong> has spectral accuracy (exponential convergence). <strong>Finite differences</strong> approximate derivatives: forward (h), central (h²), and higher-order stencils. <strong>Richardson extrapolation</strong> combines two approximations with different step sizes to cancel leading error terms.</div>
<div class="story-moral">Gauss quadrature is the superpower of numerical integration — 2n−1 degree exactness from n evaluations.</div>
</div>

<div class="section-label part3">Part 3</div>
<h2>📊 Numerical Linear Algebra and ODE Solvers</h2>
<div class="story-card">
<div class="story-label">Matrices and Time-Stepping</div>
<div class="story-title">Solving Linear Systems and Differential Equations on a Computer</div>
<div class="story-text"><strong>LU decomposition</strong> with partial pivoting solves Ax = b in O(n³). <strong>QR decomposition</strong> (Gram-Schmidt, Householder, Givens) is more stable and used for least-squares. <strong>Conjugate gradient</strong> solves symmetric positive-definite systems in O(κ^{1/2}) iterations, using only matrix-vector products — ideal for large sparse problems. <strong>GMRES</strong> handles non-symmetric systems. <strong>Power iteration</strong> finds the dominant eigenvalue; the <strong>QR algorithm</strong> finds all eigenvalues via iterative QR decompositions. <strong>Euler's method</strong> y_{n+1} = y_n + h f(t_n,y_n) is first-order O(h). <strong>Runge-Kutta methods</strong> (RK4 uses 4 stages for O(h⁴) accuracy) are the workhorses. <strong>Multistep methods</strong> (Adams-Bashforth, Adams-Moulton) use previous steps. <strong>Stiff ODEs</strong> require implicit methods (backward Euler, BDF) with unconditional stability.</div>
<div class="story-moral">Numerical computation of eigenvalues and ODE solutions is the engine behind most scientific simulations.</div>
</div>

<div class="section-label part4">Part 4</div>
<h2>⚡ Spectral Methods and Advanced Topics</h2>
<div class="story-card">
<div class="story-label">Exponential Accuracy</div>
<div class="story-title">Spectral Methods, FFT, and the Future of Numerical Computation</div>
<div class="story-text"><strong>Spectral methods</strong> expand the solution in global basis functions (Fourier series, Chebyshev polynomials) — they achieve exponential convergence for smooth solutions. The <strong>FFT</strong> (fast Fourier transform) computes the discrete Fourier transform in O(N log N), making spectral methods practical. <strong>Pseudospectral methods</strong> collocate at Chebyshev or Fourier points. <strong>Finite element methods</strong> (FEM) partition the domain into elements with local basis functions — they handle complex geometries naturally. <strong>Discontinuous Galerkin (DG)</strong> methods combine FEM and finite volume ideas for hyperbolic PDEs. <strong>Multigrid methods</strong> solve elliptic PDEs in O(N) by cycling between coarse and fine grids — optimal for large-scale problems. <strong>Adaptive mesh refinement</strong> (AMR) concentrates grid points where the solution varies rapidly.</div>
<div class="story-moral">Spectral methods give exponential convergence for smooth problems; finite elements handle the messy real world of complex geometry.</div>
</div>

